/**
\defgroup distrib_gaussian2 gaussian2 (mean, variance)
\brief Univariate normal distribution parametrized by mean and variance, with closed-form log-density derivatives and Mills-ratio cumulative tails (DLMF §7.12).

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/gaussian2.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian2_distrib "gaussian2_distrib", this module evaluates the real-line normal distribution parametrized by variance rather than standard deviation: log-density and mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and regularity metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, packed `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath reference values within 483 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 441 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

On \f$y \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ with mean \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ and standard deviation \f$\sigma \in (0, \infty)\f$, the textbook normal density is \f$f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\bigl(-\frac{(y-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\bigr)\f$. Under variance \f$v = \sigma^2 \in (0, \infty)\f$ it becomes
\f[
f(y; \mu, v) = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2\pi v}} \exp\!\left(-\frac{(y - \mu)^2}{2v}\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
Both `mu` and `v` have `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `moves_support = false`; observation regularity is `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks` and `y_kinks` report `count = 0`.

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

With \f$r = y - \mu\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, v; y) = -\frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) - \frac{1}{2}\log v - \frac{r^2}{2v}\f$. For total order \f$K = a + b \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ with \f$a\f$ derivatives in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$b\f$ in \f$v\f$ (at index \f$b\f$ in `sym_tensor<2, K>`), partial derivatives are \f$(-1)^b\bigl(\frac{(b-1)!}{2v^b} - \frac{b!\,r^2}{2v^{b+1}}\bigr)\f$ for \f$a = 0\f$, \f$(-1)^b\frac{b!\,r}{v^{b+1}}\f$ for \f$a = 1\f$, \f$(-1)^{b+1}\frac{b!}{v^{b+1}}\f$ for \f$a = 2\f$, and \f$0\f$ for \f$a > 2\f$. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) use \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

The expected Fisher information \f$I(\mu, v) = \operatorname{diag}(1/v, 1/(2v^2))\f$ is stored in `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[0] = 1.0 / v`, `v[1] = 0.0`, and `v[2] = 0.5 / (v * v)`. Its \f$K\f$-th \f$v\f$-derivative (\f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$) is \f$\operatorname{diag}\bigl(\frac{(-1)^K K!}{v^{K+1}}, \frac{(-1)^K (K+1)!}{2v^{K+2}}\bigr)\f$, while \f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish.

With \f$\sigma = \sqrt{v}\f$ and \f$z = r/\sigma\f$, cumulative functions are \f$F(y) = \Phi(z)\f$, \f$1 - F(y) = \Phi(-z)\f$, \f$\log F(y) = \log\Phi(z)\f$, \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = \log\Phi(-z)\f$, and quantile \f$F^{-1}(p) = \mu + \sqrt{v}\,\Phi^{-1}(p)\f$. Away from the vanishing tail (\f$|z| < 4\f$ or the upper-half side), \f$z\f$-derivatives from \ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1" map to \f$(\mu, v)\f$ via the power-scale chain rule \f$\sigma = w^k\f$ (\f$w = v\f$, \f$k = 1/2\f$): \f$\partial_\mu^a \partial_w^b \Lambda = \sigma^{-a} w^{-b} R_b(z)\f$, with \f$R_0 = (-\mathrm{d}/\mathrm{d}z)^a \Lambda\f$ and \f$R_{i+1} = -((k a + i)R_i + k z R_i')\f$. In the tail \f$x = -\operatorname{sgn}(z)z \ge 4\f$ (\f$t = -x\f$, \f$w_{\mathrm{tail}} = v/r^2\f$), writing \f$\log\Phi(t) = -t^2/2 - \log x - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) - w_{\mathrm{tail}} + S_2(t)\f$ keeps \f$-r^2/(2v)\f$, \f$-\log|r| + \frac{1}{2}\log v\f$, and \f$-v/r^2\f$ in closed form and chains \f$S_2(t)\f$.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against mpmath reference values at 300 and 400 bits (`tests/data/reference/distrib_gaussian23.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below; `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info_dk` match 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, and `expected_info` matches score variance within \f$10^{-11}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 483 | 730 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 441 | 670 |

## References

- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §7.12 Asymptotic Expansions (Error Functions), https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.12
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_gaussian3 gaussian3 (mean, precision)
\brief Univariate normal distribution parametrized by mean and precision, with closed-form log-density derivatives and Mills-ratio cumulative tails (DLMF §7.12).

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/gaussian3.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::gaussian3_distrib "gaussian3_distrib", this module evaluates the real-line normal distribution parametrized by precision \f$\tau = 1/\sigma^2\f$: log-density and mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and regularity metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references within 301 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 156 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

Substituting precision \f$\tau = 1/\sigma^2 \in (0, \infty)\f$ into the textbook normal density \f$f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sigma\sqrt{2\pi}}\exp\bigl(-\frac{(y-\mu)^2}{2\sigma^2}\bigr)\f$, with mean \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ and standard deviation \f$\sigma \in (0, \infty)\f$, gives
\f[
f(y; \mu, \tau) = \sqrt{\frac{\tau}{2\pi}} \exp\!\left(-\frac{\tau (y - \mu)^2}{2}\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
Both `mu` and `tau` have `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `moves_support = false`; `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks` and `y_kinks` report `count = 0`.

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

With \f$r = y - \mu\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, \tau; y) = -\frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) + \frac{1}{2}\log\tau - \frac{\tau r^2}{2}\f$. For total order \f$K = a + b \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ with \f$a\f$ derivatives in \f$\mu\f$ and \f$b\f$ in \f$\tau\f$ (at index \f$b\f$ in `sym_tensor<2, K>`), partial derivatives are \f$\frac{1}{2\tau} - \frac{r^2}{2}\f$ (\f$a = 0, b = 1\f$), \f$\frac{1}{2}(-1)^{b-1}\frac{(b-1)!}{\tau^b}\f$ (\f$a = 0, b \ge 2\f$), \f$\tau r\f$ (\f$a = 1, b = 0\f$), \f$r\f$ (\f$a = 1, b = 1\f$), \f$-\tau\f$ (\f$a = 2, b = 0\f$), \f$-1\f$ (\f$a = 2, b = 1\f$), and \f$0\f$ for \f$a \in \{1, 2\}, b \ge 2\f$ or \f$a > 2\f$. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) use \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

The expected Fisher information \f$I(\mu, \tau) = \operatorname{diag}(\tau, 1/(2\tau^2))\f$ is stored in `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[0] = tau`, `v[1] = 0.0`, and `v[2] = 0.5 / (tau * tau)`. Its \f$K\f$-th \f$\tau\f$-derivative (\f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$) is \f$\operatorname{diag}(1, -1/\tau^3)\f$ for \f$K = 1\f$ and \f$\operatorname{diag}\bigl(0, (-1)^K(K+1)!/(2\tau^{K+2})\bigr)\f$ for \f$K \ge 2\f$, while \f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish.

With \f$\sigma = 1/\sqrt{\tau}\f$ and \f$z = r\sqrt{\tau}\f$, cumulative values \f$F(y) = \Phi(z)\f$, \f$1 - F(y) = \Phi(-z)\f$, and their logarithms match \ref distrib_gaussian1 "gaussian1", and `quantile(p)` evaluates \f$\mu + \Phi^{-1}(p)/\sqrt{\tau}\f$. Away from the vanishing tail, \f$z\f$-derivatives map to \f$(\mu, \tau)\f$ via the power-scale chain rule with \f$w = \tau\f$ and \f$k = -1/2\f$. In the tail \f$x = -\operatorname{sgn}(z)z \ge 4\f$ (\f$t = -x\f$, \f$w_{\mathrm{tail}} = 1/(\tau r^2)\f$), decomposing \f$\log\Phi(t) = -t^2/2 - \log x - \frac{1}{2}\log(2\pi) - w_{\mathrm{tail}} + S_2(t)\f$ keeps \f$-\tau r^2/2\f$, \f$-\log|r| - \frac{1}{2}\log\tau\f$, and \f$-1/(\tau r^2)\f$ in closed form and chains \f$S_2(t)\f$.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against mpmath reference values at 300 and 400 bits (`tests/data/reference/distrib_gaussian23.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below; `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info_dk` match 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, and `expected_info` matches score variance within \f$10^{-11}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 301 | 460 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 156 | 240 |

## References

- A. Meurer and others (2017), SymPy: symbolic computing in Python, PeerJ Computer Science, 3, e103. https://doi.org/10.7717/peerj-cs.103
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §7.12 Asymptotic Expansions (Error Functions), https://dlmf.nist.gov/7.12
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_cauchy Cauchy (location, scale)
\brief Univariate Cauchy distribution in location and scale, with Chebyshev-like rational derivatives, Riccati log-cumulative ODE, and far-tail monomial series.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/cauchy.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::cauchy_distrib "cauchy_distrib", this module evaluates the Cauchy distribution's log-density, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and regularity metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references within 59 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 41 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

The textbook Cauchy density (Johnson and others, 1994) uses median and mode \f$x_0 \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ (mean and variance are undefined) and half-width at half-maximum \f$\gamma \in (0, \infty)\f$; Laplace writes \f$\mu = x_0\f$ (`mu`) and \f$\sigma = \gamma\f$ (`sigma`), so that the density is
\f[
f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\pi\sigma \left(1 + \left(\frac{y - \mu}{\sigma}\right)^2\right)}, \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
Both parameters have `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `moves_support = false`; `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks` and `y_kinks` report `count = 0`.

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

With \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, \sigma; y) = -\log(\pi\sigma) - \log(1 + z^2)\f$. Setting \f$\theta = \operatorname{atan2}(1, z)\f$ and \f$w = 1/(1 + z^2)\f$, the sequence \f$u_j(z) = \operatorname{Re}((z + i)^{-j}) = (1 + z^2)^{-j/2}\cos(j\theta)\f$ obeys \f$u_0 = 1\f$, \f$u_1 = z w\f$, and \f$u_{j+1} = w(2z u_j - u_{j-1})\f$, giving \f$z\f$-derivatives \f$\Lambda^{(j)}(z) = 2(-1)^j (j - 1)!\, u_j(z)\f$ of \f$\Lambda(z) = -\log(1 + z^2)\f$. These map to \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ by the location-scale chain rule, adding \f$(-1)^K (K - 1)!\,\sigma^{-K}\f$ to the pure \f$\sigma\f$ component. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) use \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

The expected Fisher information \f$I(\mu, \sigma) = \operatorname{diag}(1/(2\sigma^2), 1/(2\sigma^2))\f$ is stored with `v[0] = v[2] = 0.5 / (sigma * sigma)` and `v[1] = 0.0`, with \f$K\f$-th \f$\sigma\f$-derivative \f$\frac{(-1)^K (K + 1)!}{2\sigma^{K + 2}} I_2\f$ (\f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish).

The distribution functions are \f$F(y) = \frac{1}{2} + \frac{1}{\pi}\arctan z = \frac{1}{\pi}\operatorname{atan2}(1, -z)\f$ and \f$1 - F(y) = \frac{1}{\pi}\operatorname{atan2}(1, z)\f$, with `log_cdf` and `log_ccdf` using `log1p` on the upper-half side and `quantile(p)` evaluating \f$\mu + \sigma\tan(\pi(p - 1/2))\f$ via cotangents near the endpoints. On the region where \f$z < 2\f$ (for \f$\log F\f$) or \f$z > -2\f$ (for \f$\log(1 - F)\f$), \f$\Lambda(z) = \log(\theta(z)/\pi)\f$ obeys the Riccati ODE \f$q' = -q^2 - p q\f$ with \f$q = \Lambda' = -w/\theta\f$, \f$p(z) = 2z w\f$, \f$p^{(i)} = 2(-1)^i i!\, u_{i+1}\f$, \f$q^{(j+1)} = -\sum_{i=0}^j \binom{j}{i}(q^{(i)} + p^{(i)})q^{(j-i)}\f$, and \f$\log F(z) = \Lambda(-z)\f$. On the opposite far tail (\f$z \ge 2\f$ for \f$\log F\f$, \f$z \le -2\f$ for \f$\log(1 - F)\f$, where the log-probability tends to zero), substituting \f$s = 1/|z|\f$ avoids cancellation in \f$z\f$-polynomials via a 100-term monomial series \f$\Lambda = \log\bigl(1 - \frac{1}{\pi}\arctan s\bigr) = \sum_{n=1}^\infty d_n s^n\f$, giving \f$\partial_\mu^a \partial_\sigma^b \Lambda = \sigma^{-K}\kappa^a \sum_{n=1}^{100} d_n (n)^{\overline{a}} (n)^{\underline{b}} |z|^{-(n + a)}\f$ with \f$\kappa = \operatorname{sgn}(z)\f$.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references (`tests/data/reference/distrib_cauchy_logistic.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below. `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info` derivatives agree with 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, while expected information matches score-variance quadrature within \f$10^{-10}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 59 | 90 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 41 | 62 |

## References

- N. L. Johnson, S. Kotz and N. Balakrishnan (1994), Continuous Univariate Distributions, Volume 1, 2nd edition, Wiley.
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_logistic Logistic (location, scale)
\brief Univariate logistic distribution in location and scale, with logistic polynomial derivatives for log-density and log-cumulatives.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/logistic.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::logistic_distrib "logistic_distrib", this module evaluates the logistic distribution's log-density, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and regularity metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references within 108 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 44.4 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

The textbook logistic density (Johnson and others, 1995) on \f$y \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ uses location \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ and scale \f$s \in (0, \infty)\f$ (with mean \f$\mu\f$ and variance \f$\pi^2 s^2/3\f$); Laplace writes \f$\sigma = s\f$ (`sigma`) and `mu` for \f$\mu\f$, so that the density is
\f[
f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{\exp\!\left(-\frac{y - \mu}{\sigma}\right)}{\sigma \left(1 + \exp\!\left(-\frac{y - \mu}{\sigma}\right)\right)^2}, \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
Both parameters have `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `moves_support = false`; `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks` and `y_kinks` report `count = 0`.

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

With \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$, \f$p = F(z) = 1/(1 + e^{-z})\f$, and \f$q = 1 - p\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, \sigma; y) = -\log\sigma - z - 2\log(1 + e^{-z}) = -\log\sigma - |z| - 2\log(1 + e^{-|z|})\f$, which avoids overflow for all real \f$z\f$. Defining \f$s_k = p^{(k)}(z)\f$ with \f$s_0 = p\f$, \f$s_1 = p q\f$, and \f$s_{k+1} = (q - p)s_k - \sum_{i=1}^{k-1}\binom{k}{i} s_i s_{k-i}\f$, the \f$z\f$-derivatives of \f$\Lambda_f = \log(p q)\f$ are \f$\Lambda_f' = q - p\f$ and \f$\Lambda_f^{(j)} = -2 s_{j-1}\f$ for \f$j \ge 2\f$. These map to \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ by the location-scale chain rule, adding \f$(-1)^K (K - 1)!\,\sigma^{-K}\f$ to the pure \f$\sigma\f$ component. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) use \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

The expected Fisher information \f$I(\mu, \sigma) = \operatorname{diag}\bigl(\frac{1}{3\sigma^2}, \frac{\pi^2 + 3}{9\sigma^2}\bigr)\f$ is stored with `v[0] = 1.0 / (3.0 * sigma * sigma)`, `v[1] = 0.0`, and \f$v_2 = (\pi^2 + 3)/(9\sigma^2)\f$, with \f$K\f$-th \f$\sigma\f$-derivative \f$\frac{(-1)^K (K + 1)!}{\sigma^{K + 2}}\operatorname{diag}\bigl(\frac{1}{3}, \frac{\pi^2 + 3}{9}\bigr)\f$ (\f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish).

Cumulative functions are \f$F(y) = 1/(1 + e^{-z})\f$, \f$1 - F(y) = 1/(1 + e^z)\f$, \f$\log F(y) = -\log(1 + e^{-z})\f$, \f$\log(1 - F(y)) = -\log(1 + e^z)\f$, and `quantile(p)` \f$= \mu + \sigma\log(p/(1 - p))\f$. Because \f$(\log F)' = q\f$, \f$(\log(1 - F))' = -p\f$, and both share \f$j\f$-th \f$z\f$-derivative \f$-s_{j-1}\f$ for \f$j \ge 2\f$, their parameter derivatives share the same location-scale chain rule with no cancellation.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references (`tests/data/reference/distrib_cauchy_logistic.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below. `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info_dk` agree with 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, while expected information matches score-variance quadrature within \f$10^{-10}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 108 | 162 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 44.4 | 67 |

## References

- N. L. Johnson, S. Kotz and N. Balakrishnan (1995), Continuous Univariate Distributions, Volume 2, 2nd edition, Wiley.
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_gumbel Gumbel
\brief Univariate Gumbel (type-I maximum) distribution in mode and scale, with Laguerre log-density derivatives and Bernoulli series for the complementary log-cdf.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/gumbel.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::gumbel_distrib "gumbel_distrib", this module evaluates the Gumbel (maximum) distribution's log-density, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and regularity metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references within 19.1 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 394 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

The textbook Gumbel density (Gumbel, 1958) uses mode \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ (not the mean \f$\mu + \gamma\beta\f$, with variance \f$\pi^2\beta^2/6\f$) and scale \f$\beta \in (0, \infty)\f$; Laplace writes \f$\sigma = \beta\f$ (`sigma`) and `mu` for \f$\mu\f$, so that, with \f$z = (y - \mu)/\sigma\f$, the density is
\f[
f(y; \mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sigma} \exp\!\left(-\left(z + e^{-z}\right)\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
Both parameters have `regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `moves_support = false`; `y_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, and `param_kinks` and `y_kinks` report `count = 0`.

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

The log-density \f$\ell(\mu, \sigma; y) = -\log\sigma - z - e^{-z}\f$ separates into three closed-form parts for total order \f$K = a + b \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$: \f$-z\f$ contributes \f$-(-1)^b b!\, z\sigma^{-b}\f$ (\f$a = 0\f$), \f$(-1)^b b!\,\sigma^{-(b+1)}\f$ (\f$a = 1\f$), and \f$0\f$ (\f$a \ge 2\f$); \f$-e^{-z}\f$ contributes \f$-(-1)^b b!\,\sigma^{-K} e^{-z} L_b^{(a-1)}(z)\f$ via generalized Laguerre forward recurrence (DLMF §18.9); and \f$-\log\sigma\f$ contributes \f$(-1)^b (b - 1)!\,\sigma^{-b}\f$ for \f$a = 0, b \ge 1\f$. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) use \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

The expected Fisher information \f$I(\mu, \sigma) = \frac{1}{\sigma^2}\begin{pmatrix} 1 & \gamma - 1 \\ \gamma - 1 & (1 - \gamma)^2 + \frac{\pi^2}{6}\end{pmatrix}\f$ (where \f$\gamma\f$ is the Euler–Mascheroni constant) is non-diagonal (Gumbel, 1958) and stored in `sym_tensor<2, 2>` with `v[0] = 1.0 / (sigma * sigma)`, `v[1] = (gamma - 1.0) / (sigma * sigma)`, and \f$v_2 = \bigl((1 - \gamma)^2 + \pi^2/6\bigr)/\sigma^2\f$. Its \f$K\f$-th \f$\sigma\f$-derivative scales the constant matrix by \f$\frac{(-1)^K (K + 1)!}{\sigma^{K + 2}}\f$, while \f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish.

Cumulative functions are \f$F(y) = \exp(-e^{-z})\f$, \f$1 - F(y) = 1 - \exp(-e^{-z})\f$, \f$\log F(y) = -e^{-z}\f$ (whose derivatives equal the exponential term of \f$\ell\f$), and `quantile(p)` \f$= \mu - \sigma\log(-\log p)\f$ (using `log1p(p - 1.0)` for \f$p > 1/2\f$). For \f$\log(1 - F(y))\f$, when \f$z \ge 0\f$ the module evaluates \f$-z + H(e^{-z})\f$ with Bernoulli series \f$H(u) = \log((1 - e^{-u})/u) = \sum_{k=1}^\infty h_k u^k\f$ (\f$h_1 = -1/2\f$, \f$h_{2n} = \frac{B_{2n}}{2n(2n)!}\f$) (DLMF §24.2), and for \f$z < 0\f$, \f$\log(1 - F) = -\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{e^{-n e^{-z}}}{n}\f$ is differentiated in \f$z\f$ via polynomials \f$P_j(w)\f$ of \f$e^{-w}\f$ (\f$w = n e^{-z}\f$), then mapped to \f$(\mu, \sigma)\f$ by the location-scale chain rule.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references (`tests/data/reference/distrib_gumbel_laplace2.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below. `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info_dk` agree with 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, while expected information matches score-variance quadrature within \f$10^{-10}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 19.1 | 30 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 394 | 600 |

## References

- E. J. Gumbel (1958), Statistics of Extremes. https://doi.org/10.7312/gumb92958
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §18.9 Recurrence Relations and Derivatives (Classical Orthogonal Polynomials), https://dlmf.nist.gov/18.9
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §24.2 Definitions and Generating Functions (Bernoulli and Euler Polynomials), https://dlmf.nist.gov/24.2
*/

/**
\defgroup distrib_laplace2 laplace2 (location, rate)
\brief Univariate Laplace (double-exponential) distribution parametrized by location and rate, with symmetric kink convention and Laguerre-transformed tail series.

Defined in `<laplace/distrib/laplace2.hpp>` as \ref laplace::distrib::laplace2_distrib "laplace2_distrib", this module evaluates the Laplace distribution parametrized by inverse scale (rate \f$\lambda = 1/b\f$): log-density, mixed observation-parameter derivatives, expected Fisher information and its derivatives, cumulative and complementary probabilities with log-parameter derivatives, quantiles, and kink metadata. Functions are marked `LAPLACE_HD` for host and Kokkos device execution; see \ref distrib_core for the shared interface, `sym_tensor<2, K>` storage, and Kokkos usage. Cumulative derivatives match 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references within 5.64 scaled ulp (`log_cdf`) and 24.8 scaled ulp (`log_ccdf`).

## Classic form and regularity

The textbook Laplace density (Kotz and others, 2001) with location \f$\mu \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$ (`mu`) and scale \f$b \in (0, \infty)\f$ is \f$f(y; \mu, b) = \frac{1}{2b}\exp\bigl(-\frac{|y - \mu|}{b}\bigr)\f$. Substituting rate \f$\lambda = 1/b \in (0, \infty)\f$ (`lambda`) gives
\f[
f(y; \mu, \lambda) = \frac{\lambda}{2} \exp\!\left(-\lambda |y - \mu|\right), \qquad y \in (-\infty, \infty).
\f]
This is the same law as \ref distrib_laplace1 "laplace1_distrib" with \f$\sigma = 1/\lambda\f$. On \f$y \in (-\infty, \infty)\f$, rate \f$\lambda\f$ is infinitely smooth (`regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `cdf_regularity = infinitely_smooth`, `moves_support = false`, `param_kinks(1, y)` has `count = 0`), while at \f$\mu = y\f$ location and observation have a kink (`regularity = 0`, `y_regularity = 0`, `cdf_regularity = 1`, matching \ref distrib_laplace1 "laplace1") reported by `param_kinks(0, y)` (\f$\mu = y\f$) and `y_kinks()` (\f$y = \mu\f$).

## Log-density, Fisher information, and cumulative functions

With \f$r = y - \mu\f$ and \f$s = \operatorname{sgn}(r)\f$, the log-density is \f$\ell(\mu, \lambda; y) = \log\lambda - \log 2 - \lambda|r|\f$. Away from the kink (\f$r \ne 0\f$), partials of order \f$K = a + b \in \{1, \dots, 6\}\f$ are \f$1/\lambda - |r|\f$ (\f$a = 0, b = 1\f$), \f$(-1)^{b-1}(b - 1)!/\lambda^b\f$ (\f$a = 0, b \ge 2\f$), \f$\lambda s\f$ (\f$a = 1, b = 0\f$), \f$s\f$ (\f$a = 1, b = 1\f$), and \f$0\f$ for \f$a = 1, b \ge 2\f$ or \f$a \ge 2\f$, adopting the symmetric convention \f$\operatorname{sgn}(0) = 0\f$ at \f$r = 0\f$. Mixed derivatives `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>(y)` (\f$J + K \le 6\f$) satisfy \f$\partial/\partial y = -\partial/\partial\mu\f$.

From the score variance (Lehmann and Casella, 1998), the expected Fisher information is \f$I(\mu, \lambda) = \operatorname{diag}(\lambda^2, \lambda^{-2})\f$ (`v[0] = lambda * lambda`, `v[1] = 0.0`, `v[2] = 1.0 / (lambda * lambda)`). Its \f$K\f$-th \f$\lambda\f$-derivative (\f$K \in \{1, \dots, 4\}\f$) is \f$\operatorname{diag}(2\lambda, -2/\lambda^3)\f$ for \f$K = 1\f$, \f$\operatorname{diag}(2, 6/\lambda^4)\f$ for \f$K = 2\f$, and \f$\operatorname{diag}\bigl(0, (-1)^K(K + 1)!/\lambda^{K + 2}\bigr)\f$ for \f$K \ge 3\f$, while \f$\mu\f$-derivatives vanish.

With \f$z = \lambda(y - \mu)\f$, cumulative values are piecewise exponentials and `quantile(p)` returns \f$\mu + \frac{1}{\lambda}\log(2p)\f$ for \f$p < 1/2\f$ and \f$\mu - \frac{1}{\lambda}\log(2 - 2p)\f$ for \f$p \ge 1/2\f$. For cumulative derivatives where the log-probability tends to zero (\f$z \ge 1/4\f$ for \f$\log F\f$, \f$z \le -1/4\f$ for \f$\log(1 - F)\f$, with \f$q = |y - \mu|\f$ and \f$u = n\lambda q\f$), the module expands \f$\log\bigl(1 - \frac{1}{2}e^{-\lambda q}\bigr) = -\sum_{n=1}^\infty \frac{2^{-n}}{n}e^{-n\lambda q}\f$ and transforms negative-order Laguerre polynomials via DLMF 18.5.12, \f$b!\, L_b^{(a-b)}(u) = (-u)^{b-a} a!\, L_a^{(b-a)}(u)\f$, so all coefficients remain non-negative. On \f$z < 1/4\f$ (\f$\log F\f$) and \f$z > -1/4\f$ (\f$\log(1 - F)\f$), \f$z\f$-derivatives from \ref distrib_laplace1 "laplace1" map to \f$(\mu, \lambda)\f$ via the power-scale chain rule with \f$w = \lambda\f$ and \f$k = -1\f$.

## Accuracy

Measured on the gcc build (Kokkos Serial and OpenMP) against 300-bit and 400-bit mpmath references (`tests/data/reference/distrib_gumbel_laplace2.csv`), scaled-ulp errors (see \ref accuracy and \ref distrib_core) satisfy the bounds below. `logpdf_dk`, `logpdf_dy_dk<J, K>()`, and `expected_info_dk` agree with 5-point finite differences within \f$10^{-8}\f$ relative tolerance, while expected information matches score-variance quadrature within \f$10^{-12}\f$.

| Quantity | Max error (scaled ulp) | Test bound (scaled ulp) |
|---|---|---|
| `log_cdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 5.64 | 8.5 |
| `log_ccdf` derivatives (\f$K = 1 \dots 6\f$) | 24.8 | 37 |

## Proximal operator

The member `prox_y(v, t)` returns the proximal operator of the law in \f$y\f$ (see \ref penalty_prox "Proximal operators"). Here \f$v\f$ is the point and \f$t > 0\f$ the step. With \f$u = v - \mu\f$ and \f$k = t\lambda\f$, it equals \f$\mu\f$ when \f$|u| \le k\f$, and \f$\mu + (u - k)\f$ or \f$\mu + (u + k)\f$ otherwise, computed so that the result stays on the same side of \f$\mu\f$ as \f$v\f$.

## References

- S. Kotz, T. J. Kozubowski and K. Podgórski (2001), The Laplace Distribution and Generalizations. https://doi.org/10.1007/978-1-4612-0173-1
- E. L. Lehmann and G. Casella (1998), Theory of Point Estimation, 2nd edition, Springer. https://doi.org/10.1007/b98854
- The mpmath development team (2023), mpmath: a Python library for arbitrary-precision floating-point arithmetic, version 1.3.0, https://mpmath.org
- NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §18.5 Explicit Representations (Classical Orthogonal Polynomials), https://dlmf.nist.gov/18.5
*/
